Loomglass

Ingredients: POST-PUNK + CINEMATIC, EPIC & ORCHESTRAL + Vibraphone
Creative Catalyst: Solitary lighthouse keepers broadcast brooding, symphonic pirate transmissions to icebound trawlers across endless polar nights.

Loomglass is a stark, oceanic fusion where the mechanical pulse and serrated basslines of late-1970s coldwave collide with the sweeping, catastrophic grandeur of a full orchestral chamber. Suspended above this collision is the vibraphone—played with hard phenolic mallets or bowed with horsehair—producing an icy, luminous ring that cuts through towering brass swells and motorik drum patterns like an arc-light piercing an arctic gale.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 115–132 BPM. A relentless, four-on-the-floor motorik kick and rigid 16th-note hi-hat drive borrowed from minimalist post-punk, frequently disrupted or counter-accented by the thunderous, rolling cadences of symphonic timpani.
- Harmonic Palette: Stark Dorian and Aeolian modal progressions. Cold minor triads on distorted bass guitars resolve unexpectedly into lush, sweeping orchestral suspensions (sus2, sus4) and dramatic, brass-heavy Phrygian descents.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Fresnel Gating & Plate Saturation: Snare drums and vibraphones routed together through cavernous, metallic plate reverbs that abruptly gate shut on the offbeat, mimicking the mechanical turn of an occulting lighthouse lamp.
  2. Shortwave Vocal Masking: Baritone lead vocals tracked through carbon-capsule marine microphones, band-passed with resonant high-frequency hiss to evoke deep-sea emergency broadcasts.
  3. Tidal Sidechain Ducking: The entire symphonic string and brass section dynamically ducks behind the rigid post-punk bassline, rising into violent orchestral swells the exact second the bass rests.
- Dynamics: Songs begin in austere solitude—just a dry, chugging bass guitar and brittle vibraphone chime—before steadily stacking cellos, contrabasses, and distant horn fanfares into overwhelming, cinematic tidal waves that crash down, recede to silence, and rebuild.

Scene & Origin Lore
The genre emerged in the late 1970s along the storm-battered rim of the Svalbard archipelago and the Lofoten shelf. Isolated keepers running maritime relay stations—facing six months of unyielding polar dark—began hauling portable East German vibraphones and four-track reel-to-reels up into the high glass lantern galleries. Splicing classical orchestral tapes with jagged bass loops played on cheap imported guitars, they broadcast unmetered pirate symphonies over low-frequency distress frequencies (2182 kHz) to keep foreign cod crews and freighters awake. Mainland radio enthusiasts and art-school dropouts in Tromsø and Bergen intercepted the eerie signals on coastal shortwave gear, quickly canonizing the sound. The resulting subculture dressed in heavy, salt-stiffened oiled wool reefer jackets, cracked industrial brass safety goggles, and mariner boots, congregating in unheated dockside warehouses where full string quartets and heavy orchestral timpani were dragged onstage alongside chorus-drenched Rickenbacker basses.

Typical Instruments: Vibraphone (struck with phenolic mallets and bowed with double-bass bows), Fender VI and Rickenbacker 4001 bass guitars, angular electric guitars running through vintage chorus/flanger pedals, orchestral string ensemble (heavy emphasis on low cellos and bowed contrabasses), French horns, muted trumpets, timpani, and tight, dry acoustic drums.

For Fans Of: The Cure (specifically Disintegration era), Scott Walker (late orchestral period), Tindersticks, Swans.

