Track 1: Cullet Glass Bites the Barents
- Story & Context: During a force-ten gale in November 1981 at Sørkapp Lighthouse, keeper Torstein Holte watched a rogue breaker shatter the lantern room's storm glazing, showering the transmitter console in freezing brine and sharp chunks of cullet. Rather than cutting the transmission, Holte jammed a brass lead weight onto the sustain pedal of his vibraphone, routed his overdriven bass guitar into the distress transmitter, and belted a rhythmic dirge across the maritime band that the nearby trawler Vestis used to navigate clear of the coastal shoals. The recording features the actual clatter of broken glass vibrating against the vibraphone frame as Holte howls the opening declaration, "Every tooth of the sea turns to glass tonight," creating an accidental masterpiece of survivalist post-punk that birthed the scene.
