Track 3: Annealing in Gated Reverb
- Story & Context: Long after the Vardø coastal stations were automated and emptied of human keepers, local drift-netters reported strange rhythmic thuds and sweeping symphonic chords bleeding through their ship hulls whenever they drifted near the decommissioned iron towers. Dockside lore claimed that the concrete foundations and cast-iron frames had absorbed fifteen years of high-volume pirate broadcasts, slowly releasing the trapped resonance into the sub-zero seabed like hot glass cooling inside an annealing kiln. In 1993, an independent coastal hydrophone crew finally recorded the seabed frequency, uncovering an unreleased, ghostlike Loomglass rhythm section buried in the acoustic wash, punctuated by a distant, treated baritone murmur: "The light keeps running even when the blood cools down."
